WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.438 --> 00:00:03.021
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.163
- Hi,

00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:06.460
I'm Kristof Zyskowski,

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.480
and we are in the
Environmental Science Center

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.890
next door to the Peabody Museum building

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:14.840
and we are in the Bird
and Mammal Collections

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.123
of the Peabody Museum.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.600
Here,

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.110
we actually have cabinets
full of bird skins.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:26.860
We have about 70% of all
bird species of the world,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.320
represented in about 150,000 specimens.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.700
And here,

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.870
let's look at what they are like.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:38.700
So here we are in the drawer of Wrens,

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.740
specifically Cactus Wren,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.930
and here I'm holding a specimen

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.750
collected in Tucson, Arizona

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:49.750
by David Parsons on 6th April, 1958.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.630
So each specimen is a
point in time and space

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:57.320
and there's a description
of the internal anatomy.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.610
These are extremely valuable
to scientists and used,

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:01.443
not only by scientists,

00:01:01.443 --> 00:01:06.443
but also by artists and for
various studies of genetics

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:07.920
and morphology.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.900
And the reason we have multiple
specimens of each species

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:11.733
is that...

00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:14.090
It's because to do any
statistical analysis

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.150
and measurements,

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.450
you need more than one,

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.040
and you can also see
that there is variation.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.130
Not a single bird here is
identical to the other.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:23.380
We actually need to have series.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:27.070
And if we move to some
species like House Wren,

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.490
where there are multiple
geographic subspecies,

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:31.940
you can actually see maybe

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.023
that these two are quite different.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:43.250
So this one here is from
Granada Island in the Caribbean,

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.560
and this one here from Columbia,

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.810
from Santander in the Andes.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.660
So the reason for collecting
multiple specimens

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.550
is for us to be able to
understand things like

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:55.560
geographic variation

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.463
and sexual and seasonal
variation in these birds.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.750
So here we are with some
additional examples of specimens

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.860
from the Peabody Museum Collections.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:11.200
So in addition to preserving study skins,

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.200
we also occasionally preserve
the entire bird in ethanol.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.900
And these kind of anatomical
specimens can be used

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.430
for visualizing skeleton
and internal organs using

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:27.660
current technologies like CAT scanning,

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.710
computed tomography.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:34.710
Occasionally we also generate
specimens of skeletons.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.410
So here I have an example.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.243
This is,

00:02:38.243 --> 00:02:39.640
you may guess,

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:40.693
a Flamingo.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:43.240
And in this case,

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.350
the skeleton is disarticulated,

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:48.290
means every bone detached

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:52.050
and available for making measurements

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.973
and photograph individually.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.480
In addition for the exhibits,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.680
we have sometimes prepare
skeletons that are articulated.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:02.960
And in this case here,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.447
there is a Great Auk skeleton,

00:03:06.447 --> 00:03:10.570
put together anatomically correctly.

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.003
And besides these,

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.910
there's a big collection
of nests and eggs.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.620
So here's an example of an egg set.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.090
So, nicely padded.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:26.480
And these are not your entire eggs,

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:27.560
but emptied eggs.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.970
So you can see these holes

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.773
in the equatorial part of the egg.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.540
And this simply is an egg shell

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.820
that is measurable and can be studied.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:41.500
And nests.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.080
We have also a large collection of nests,

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.004
and here's an example of an Oriole nest

00:03:48.004 --> 00:03:50.633
suspended from a fork
like a little hammock.

00:03:51.966 --> 00:03:53.620
And if you maybe zoom in,

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.480
you can perhaps see that there is

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.170
a lot of fishing line embedded into it.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.853
There is even a hook dangling,

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.480
right here.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.190
So this bird appropriated the material

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:09.223
that found somewhere along the river.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:14.360
And in addition to all of this,

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.130
we also preserve tissue samples.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.160
This is just an empty tube to show you,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:23.160
but for every single specimen
we collect these days,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.310
we preserve a little
piece of muscle tissue

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:27.270
or some other tissue.

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.360
And these are all stored in our

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.933
liquid nitrogen vapor frozen facility.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:38.530
And what you see on the exhibits

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:41.000
is just the tip of the iceberg.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.030
So here behind me,

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.650
you have a little example,

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.330
some examples of mammals and birds

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.010
that were at some point

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:49.870
in the Peabody exhibits,

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.210
but now they're here in storage

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.402
waiting for another opportunity.

00:04:54.402 --> 00:04:56.985
(upbeat music)

